                </td>
                <td width=572 valign=center colspan=10 style="width:343.6000pt;padding:0.0000pt 5.4000pt 0.0000pt 5.4000pt ;border-left:none;
mso-border-left-alt:none;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><p><span style="font-family: arial, helvetica, sans-serif;">Medical Microbiology and Immunology is a compulsory foundational course for students in biomedicine-related majors. The first half of the course provides a systematic introduction to the core principles of immunology, including innate and adaptive immunity; antigens and the induction of immune responses; immune molecules and cells; the mechanisms underlying immune responses; and the basic concepts of immune intervention and immunotherapy. The second half of the course focuses on microbiology, with an emphasis on bacteriology and virology. Topics in basic bacteriology and virology cover the morphology and structure, growth and replication, genetic variation, pathogenic mechanisms and host immunity, laboratory diagnosis, and strategies for specific prevention and control of bacteria and viruses. Clinical bacteriology and virology address medically important pathogens, with particular attention to their biological characteristics, pathogenicity, host immune responses, microbiological testing, and approaches to prevention and treatment. In addition, pathogenic mycology introduces the major features of medically relevant fungi and their significance in human disease.</span></p></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="height:19.9000pt;">
